   $q$Read: "The class camping trip was canceled. A thunderstorm was in the forecast." Which choice best combines these sentences?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The class camping trip was canceled because a thunderstorm was in the forecast.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The class camping trip was canceled, although a thunderstorm was in the forecast.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contrast-for-cause"},{"text":"The class camping trip was canceled, so a thunderstorm was in the forecast.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-cause-effect"},{"text":"The class camping trip was canceled; for example, a thunderstorm was in the forecast.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"example-for-cause"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The class camping trip was canceled because a thunderstorm was in the forecast.$q$, $q$The storm forecast is the reason for the cancellation, so "because" shows the correct cause and effect. Using "so" reverses the relationship, making it sound as if canceling the trip caused the storm.$q$, 'draft', null)
